[[:db/retract 17592186409884 :answer-field/choices 17592186409841] [:db/retract 17592186409884 :answer-field/choices 17592186409857] [:db/retract 17592186409884 :answer-field/choices 17592186409858] [:db/retract 17592186409884 :answer-field/choices 17592186409847] [:db/retract 17592186409884 :answer-field/choices 17592186409850] [:db/retract 17592186409880 :answer-field/choices 17592186409873] [:db/retract 17592186409880 :answer-field/choices 17592186409843] [:db/retract 17592186409880 :answer-field/choices 17592186409849] [:db/retract 17592186409880 :answer-field/choices 17592186409870] [:db/retract 17592186409880 :answer-field/choices 17592186409871] [:db/retract 17592186409887 :answer-field/choices 17592186409864] [:db/retract 17592186409887 :answer-field/choices 17592186409846] [:db/retract 17592186409887 :answer-field/choices 17592186409848] [:db/retract 17592186409887 :answer-field/choices 17592186409867] [:db/retract 17592186409887 :answer-field/choices 17592186409869] {:db/id 17592186091945 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186420399 :question/problem "Which of the following is the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of the subset $A=\\{2,4,8\\}$ of $X=\\{0,2,4,6,8\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{2,4,8\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/85/850019897d92b0a8d3340c091cee15342be20e99e7578a9fca23f88804e3bf28.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $2,4$ and $8$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186409892 :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c60072882a6effae61da543bfddff1053ab0bcc924d12c49f5acd697b0871c4.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $b,c$ and $e$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{b,c,e\\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186409884 :answer-field/correct "answer-82a661df-e47f-572a-8fd4-59c67535d1c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5a8625f8-e0d2-5655-8966-249f3aefa53c" "answer-1d7b4811-3743-5f1c-befb-52377f1ba995" "answer-e098f432-8d04-5972-83c4-4a6611dad8b1" "a